p(M_{2}\\,|\\,\\mathbf{\\theta})=\\left[(1-f_{\\rm NS})\\ p_{\\rm WD}+f_{\\rm NS }\\ p_{\\rm NS}\\right],  where f_{\\rm NS} is the NS fraction and

p_{\\rm WD} =\\mathcal{N}(M_{2}\\,|\\,\\mu_{\\rm WD},\\sigma_{\\rm WD}^{2});\\ 0.2< \\frac{M_{2}}{{\\rm M}_{\\odot}}<1.44,  p_{\\rm NS} =\\mathcal{N}(M_{2}\\,|\\,\\mu_{\\rm NS},\\sigma_{\\rm NS}^{2});\\ 1.3< \\frac{M_{2}}{{\\rm M}_{\\odot}}<2. 

\\mathcal{N} is the (truncated, but properly normalized) normal distribution with mean \\mu and variance \\sigma^{2}; the distributions are limited to the ranges specified. To reduce the number of parameters in our model we fix \\mu_{\\rm NS} and \\sigma_{\\rm NS} to:

\\mu_{\\rm NS} =1.4\\ {\\rm M}_{\\odot},  \\sigma_{\\rm NS} =0.05\\ {\\rm M}_{\\odot},  as some NSs in binaries may be somewhat more massive than the canonical NS mass of 1.35 M{}_{\\odot}(Kiziltan et al., 2013; Smedley et al., 2014).

The probability of any particular WD having a NS companion, P_{\\rm NS}, can be computed for a given set of parameters for the M_{2} distribution:

P_{\\rm NS}=\\frac{\\int_{M_{2,\\rm min}}^{\\infty}dM_{2}\\ f_{\\rm NS}\\ p_{\\rm NS}\\ h(M_{2},m_{f},M_{1})}{p(m_{f}\\,|\\, \\mathbf{\\theta})}. 

Our companion mass model parameters are then \\mathbf{\\theta}=(\\mu_{\\rm WD},\\sigma_{\\rm WD},f_{\\rm NS}). For \\mu_{\\rm WD}, we use a uniform prior from 0.2-1.0 M{}_{\\odot}; for \\sigma_{\\rm WD}, we use a logarithmic (scale-invariant) prior over the range 0.02-2.0 M{}_{\\odot}. Finally, we use a uniform prior over the dimensionless f_{\\rm NS} from 0-1. The model parameters are summarized in Table 1.

We use a Markov Chain Monte Carlo algorithm (Goodman & Weare, 2010) to draw samples from the posterior distribution, p(\\mu_{\\rm WD},\\sigma_{\\rm WD},f_{\\rm NS}\\,|\\,\\mathbf{m}_{f},\\mathbf{M}_{1}).4 The algorithm uses an ensemble of individual "walkers" to naturally adapt to the geometry of the parameter-space being explored. We run the walkers for a burn-in period of 500 steps starting from randomly drawn initial conditions (sampled from the priors in Table 1). We then re-initialize the walkers from their positions at the end of this run and run again for 1000 steps. We remove the burn-in samples to eliminate any effects due to our choice of initial conditions.

